WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.360
Look at this line.
And now, look at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:10.320
three lines. Can you tell which of them&nbsp;
matches that first line? And how sure are you ?&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.920
Hold that thought and listen to this story.&nbsp;
Because most people can get this wrong.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.800
Once upon a time, on the eve&nbsp;
of a major religious holiday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.320
a seven year old boy was observing his&nbsp;
grandmother as she poured an extra glass of wine.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.120
When the boy asked who the glass was for,&nbsp;
she said that it was for the prophet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.800
The boy didn’t believe her, so his uncle&nbsp;
told him to just observe the glass closely.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.920
Full of expectation, the boy watched&nbsp;
the glass intently, and suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.840
he thought he saw the level&nbsp;
of wine drop just a bit!

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:05.120
In 1951, decades after the incident, our&nbsp;
boy became a pioneer in social psychology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.400
determined to rationalize&nbsp;
what actually happened that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.160
fateful night. Did some of the wine really&nbsp;
disappear, or was he made to believe that?

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.000
To find out the truth, he devised an experiment&nbsp;
to investigate whether peer pressure can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.360
strong enough to change our perception, and&nbsp;
make us believe in things that are not true.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:37.440
Solomon Asch's conformity experiment was&nbsp;
conducted with 123 white, male college students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:45.280
who were told to be part of a study in visual&nbsp;
judgment. Each student was introduced to a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:53.840
of 6-8 other students, who were, in fact, actors&nbsp;
who already knew what the experiment was about.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.760
The group was then gathered&nbsp;
in a classroom and shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.640
two cards. The first with one line,&nbsp;
the second with three lines on it.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.360
Each student was then asked to say&nbsp;
aloud which line in the second card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.840
matched that first one. The group was&nbsp;
seated in such a way that allowed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.800
actors to respond first, leaving the&nbsp;
real participant to respond last.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.880
For the first two rounds, the fake students,&nbsp;
also known as confederates, gave the correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.640
answer and the innocent student, known as the&nbsp;
subject, said whatever he thought to be true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.520
In the third round, the actors began&nbsp;
to respond with a clearly wrong answer.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.800
For the next 12 rounds, dubbed "the&nbsp;
critical trials", all the confederates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.520
would give the obviously wrong answer.
So the innocent student could either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.080
ignore the majority or ignore the truth. So&nbsp;
what did the experiment tell the researchers?

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:09.760
Upon completing 18 rounds with all 123 subjects,&nbsp;
the experiment came to the following conclusion.&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:20.320
23% of the students always gave the right answer.&nbsp;
72% conformed to the majority at least once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.600
And 5% completely succumbed to peer pressure&nbsp;
and always gave the obviously wrong answer.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.840
Asch’s work also showed that if just one&nbsp;
other acting student responded correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.960
the effect of peer pressure&nbsp;
decreased substantially.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.000
The experiment also showed that the opinions&nbsp;
of three peers are enough to influence a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.880
student to change his mind. Increasing&nbsp;
the number beyond three confederates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.080
did not increase the levels&nbsp;
of conformity among others.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.800
Or put differently; if 3 people&nbsp;
say something obviously wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:05.120
and there is no one to disagree with them, many&nbsp;
of us agree with the wrong answer and start lying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.920
due to our deeply human desire to fit in.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.440
Since then, Asch’s experiment has been&nbsp;
successfully replicated many times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.800
the effect remains significant, especially&nbsp;
among women and homogeneous peer groups.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:30.480
In 1955, Asch famously said: “The tendency&nbsp;
to conformity in our society is so strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.520
that reasonably intelligent and well-meaning young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.720
people are willing to call white,&nbsp;
black. This is a matter of concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.640
It raises questions about our ways of education&nbsp;
and about the values that guide our conduct.”

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.480
What do you think? Would you have&nbsp;
conformed or stuck to the truth?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:57.280
Do you see the Asch’s effect in your daily&nbsp;
life or in social media? And what can we as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.520
adults or educators do to give young people today&nbsp;
the strength to stand up and speak the truth?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.840
Share your thoughts in the comments below!

